 "reading": "Under the bounded pool both proportional gates fail. The narrow one (4 thresholds) runs away before 750 updates: 247,000 pulses a byte against a budget of 113,000, the price 17 and climbing to 126. The wide one (16) diverges after 1,500 updates, its price rising from 0.17 to 0.54, and scores 2.31 at 2,250. In the narrow run the pool reached 36 to 47 quanta, where a single gate step let in all of the value: the gates turned into on/off switches (wide open at 65% to 92% of cell-bytes) and 58% to 83% of the bodies sat at their bound, where they learn nothing. Under the lifting pool, which divides the gate's charge before any bound so that a loud stream closes gates, the narrow ramp still runs away (2.31, 2.44, 2.96; price 11.4; its open gates let in 87% to 94%). The wide ramp is stable: 0.088 ahead of the lifted plain base at 750 updates, 0.013 at 1,500 and 0.003 at 2,250, at the same pulses and nearly the same price (0.064 against 0.049). Its gates are graded: open at 37% to 39% of cell-bytes, they let in 41% to 53% of the value on average and are wide open at only 3% to 8%. The shunting gate with inner steps, whose open gates let in 76% to 83%, leads at 750 updates and trails by 0.037 at 1,500 and 0.063 at 2,250 under the lifting pool, as under the bounded one (0.045 and 0.066). Inner steps are worth 0.013 to 0.019 against the shunt whose steps fire, at every evaluation, so the energy tax is the smaller part of the shunt's loss. The gated cells that lost acted as switches; the gate that stays graded keeps pace.",
 "verdict": "Only the wide proportional gate under the lifting pool is stable and level with its plain base at 2,250 updates. It goes to a screen by slope, two seeds on the server and one on the laptop (proportional_gates_with_data.json). The narrow ramp is dropped, the bounded ramp is not used, and the shunting gate is not screened again."
}
